{
  "about": "Cases for the validators every host runs on what engines and views send (src/shared/engine.ts, src/shared/native.ts), shared so that each implementation (TypeScript now, Kotlin in mobile/android, Swift later) accepts and refuses exactly the same messages. tests/shared/validatorCases.test.ts runs them all, as does mobile/android's ValidatorFixtureTest. Each section names a validator and lists values it must accept (valid) and refuse (invalid). Values are JSON, with these expansions: {\"$repeat\": s, \"times\": n} is s repeated n times; {\"$bytes\": n} is n zero bytes (a Uint8Array; Kotlin's Wire envelope); {\"$list\": item, \"times\": n} is n copies of item; {\"$object\": [key, value], \"times\": n} is an object of n entries; {\"$text\": [...]} joins strings and code points (numbers); {\"$limit\": n} is the count n as a number. In $list and $object, {i} in a string becomes the index. A count n is a number or a limit's name, optionally +1 or -1; limits lists their values, which each host checks against its own constants. {\"$b64\": ...} is no expansion: it is a malformed bytes envelope, which a body must not be either way.",
